🔧 Why Are Services Needed?

Pods in Kubernetes are ephemeral—they can die and be replaced at any time. Each pod gets a unique IP, but that IP is not static. A Service provides a stable network identity (IP + DNS name) to a group of Pods selected by a label selector.

🧱 Types of Kubernetes Services

ClusterIP	-> Exposes the service on a cluster-internal IP.
NodePort	-> Exposes the service on each Node's IP at a static port.
LoadBalancer	-> Provisions an external load balancer (cloud-based).
ExternalName	-> Maps the service to an external DNS name.
Headless Service (ClusterIP: None)	-> Used for direct access to individual pods.



1. ClusterIP Service

Used for internal communication within the cluster.

🔹 Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-internal-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP

Pods with label app: my-app will be accessible internally at my-internal-service:80.



2. NodePort Service

Exposes the service on a static port on each node's IP.

🔹 Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-nodeport-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - port: 80         # Service port
      targetPort: 8080 # Container port
      nodePort: 30080  # Node IP:30080 -> Pod:8080

Now accessible as http://<NodeIP>:30080



3. LoadBalancer Service

Used in cloud environments (AWS, GCP, Azure). Provisions an external load balancer.

🔹 Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-loadbalancer-service
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080

Will be accessible via an external IP created by your cloud provider.



4. ExternalName Service

Redirects traffic to an external DNS.

🔹 Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-external-service
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: example.com

Accessing my-external-service.default.svc.cluster.local will resolve to example.com.



5. Headless Service

Used when you want to directly access individual pods (common with StatefulSets or DNS-based service discovery).

🔹 Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-headless-service
spec:
  clusterIP: None
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - port: 80

DNS will return A records of pod IPs, not a single ClusterIP.



🔄 Service Discovery in Kubernetes

Kubernetes offers service discovery through:

DNS (via kube-dns or CoreDNS)

Example: Service my-service in namespace default is accessible at:

my-service.default.svc.cluster.local



📌 Summary

ClusterIP	 -> Internal access only
NodePort	-> Exposes app on node IP and static port
LoadBalancer	-> Exposes app externally with load balancer
ExternalName	-> Points to external DNS
Headless	-> For direct pod-to-pod communication
